T.append(("A01","Astronomy","""Here's a question that sounds simple: what does a typical planetary system look like? For most of history we had exactly one example, our own, so we couldn't really answer it. Then in 1995 astronomers found a planet circling an ordinary Sun-like star, 51 Pegasi, and it was bizarre. It had roughly half the mass of Jupiter, and it went around its star in about four days. Four days! Mercury takes eighty-eight. A giant planet that close to its star had no place in the theories of the time.

Over the next few years, more of these so-called hot Jupiters turned up, one after another. So you might conclude that this is what planetary systems usually look like, and that ours is the odd one out. And for a while, a lot of people did conclude that.

Here's the catch. Think about how those planets were found. The main technique watched for a star wobbling back and forth as a planet tugged on it. Now, which planets tug hardest and fastest? Massive ones in tight orbits. A hot Jupiter makes a big wobble that repeats every few days, so you can confirm it in a single observing season. A planet like our Jupiter takes about twelve years to complete one orbit, and its wobble is far smaller. The method wasn't sampling planets. It was sampling the planets that were easiest for it to see.

When later surveys searched more systematically, hot Jupiters turned out to be rare, found around something like one Sun-like star in a hundred. They'd been overrepresented because they were conspicuous, not because they were common.

So is our solar system typical after all? I wouldn't go that far. Plenty of systems found since look nothing like ours either. The honest lesson is narrower: before you ask what's typical, ask what your instrument could have missed.""",[
 Q("What is the main point of the lecture?","medium",["Early detections made a rare type of planet look common","Hot Jupiters are the most common planets around Sun-like stars","Our solar system has now been shown to be a typical one","Giant planets usually form much closer to stars than expected"],"Early detections made a rare type of planet look common","Hot Jupiters 'had been overrepresented because they were conspicuous, not because they were common.'",["Later surveys found them around 'something like one Sun-like star in a hundred'.","The professor says 'I wouldn't go that far.'","Close-in giants turned out to be rare, which is the opposite of usual."]),
 Q("Why does the professor mention that Mercury takes eighty-eight days to orbit the Sun?","medium",["To stress how short the new planet's orbit was","To show that small planets are easiest to detect","To suggest that hot Jupiters form where Mercury is","To show that close planets were well understood"],"To stress how short the new planet's orbit was","'Four days! Mercury takes eighty-eight.' The comparison makes the four-day orbit striking.",["The method favored massive planets, not small ones.","Nothing is said about where hot Jupiters form relative to Mercury.","Such a planet 'had no place in the theories of the time'."]),
 Q("According to the professor, why could a hot Jupiter be confirmed quickly?","medium",["Its large wobble repeats every few days","It takes about twelve years to orbit its star","It tugs on its star only very slightly","It sits far from the glare of its star"],"Its large wobble repeats every few days","'A hot Jupiter makes a big wobble that repeats every few days, so you can confirm it in a single observing season.'",["Twelve years is the orbit of a planet like our Jupiter.","Massive close planets 'tug hardest'.","A hot Jupiter orbits very close to its star."]),
 Q("What does the professor imply about whether our solar system is typical?","hard",["Current evidence does not settle whether it is typical or not","It is clearly typical now that hot Jupiters are known to be rare","It is clearly unusual because most systems have hot Jupiters","It cannot be compared with others until new methods exist"],"Current evidence does not settle whether it is typical or not","'So is our solar system typical after all? I wouldn't go that far. Plenty of systems found since look nothing like ours either.'",["The professor explicitly declines this conclusion.","Hot Jupiters turned out to be rare, about one star in a hundred.","Comparison is already happening: 'plenty of systems found since' are compared with ours."])]))
T.append(("A02","Anthropology","""In the early twentieth century, the anthropologist Bronisław Malinowski spent several years in the Trobriand Islands, off the coast of New Guinea, and he described an exchange system that seemed, at first, to make no economic sense. It's called the Kula.

Here's how it works. Men sail between islands, sometimes across long stretches of open sea, to exchange two kinds of objects: long necklaces of red shell, and white shell armbands. And the two travel in opposite directions around a great ring of islands. Necklaces move one way, roughly clockwise, and armbands move the other. When you receive a necklace, you'll eventually pass it on to a partner farther along the ring. You don't keep it. Some of these objects are hardly ever worn.

So think about that. People risk dangerous voyages to get things they can't use and won't keep. Why?

Malinowski's answer was that the value lies in holding and passing on, not in owning. A famous necklace carries its history with it, the names of the people who've held it, and having it pass through your hands raises your standing. More importantly, Kula partnerships last for life. A man has fixed partners on other islands, and those relationships give him hosts, allies, and safe passage in places where he'd otherwise be a stranger.

Now, here's the twist that's easy to miss. Alongside the ceremonial exchange, ordinary trade went on: food, pottery, tools, bartered with haggling, the kind of bargaining the Kula itself was supposed to rule out. So the Kula wasn't a replacement for practical trade. It was more like the framework that made practical trade possible, a network of trust stretched across the sea.

The broader lesson, and economic anthropologists still argue about it, is that exchange doesn't have to be about getting things. Sometimes it's about building the relationships that everything else depends on.""",[
 Q("What is the lecture mainly about?","medium",["How an exchange that looks useless serves social ends","Why shell valuables slowly lost their value over time","How Trobriand islanders built boats for long voyages","Why Malinowski's account of the Kula proved mistaken"],"How an exchange that looks useless serves social ends","The Kula 'seemed, at first, to make no economic sense', but it builds standing, lifelong partnerships and trust.",["A famous necklace gains value from its history; no loss of value is described.","Voyages are mentioned, but boatbuilding is not discussed.","The professor presents Malinowski's answer as the explanation, not as an error."]),
 Q("According to the professor, what does a man eventually do with a necklace he receives?","medium",["He passes it on to a partner along the ring","He keeps it as a lasting sign of his own rank","He hands it back to the partner who gave it","He wears it every day until it is worn out"],"He passes it on to a partner along the ring","'When you receive a necklace, you'll eventually pass it on to a partner farther along the ring. You don't keep it.'",["'You don't keep it.' Standing comes from it passing through your hands.","It moves on 'farther along the ring', not back.","'Some of these objects are hardly ever worn.'"]),
 Q("Why does the professor mention food, pottery, and tools?","hard",["To show that ordinary trade went on beside the Kula","To list the objects passed around the Kula ring","To show that the Kula replaced bargaining over goods","To explain why the sea voyages were so dangerous"],"To show that ordinary trade went on beside the Kula","'Alongside the ceremonial exchange, ordinary trade went on: food, pottery, tools, bartered with haggling.'",["The Kula objects are the necklaces and armbands.","'The Kula wasn't a replacement for practical trade.'","These goods are not connected to the danger of the voyages."]),
 Q("What can be inferred about haggling in the Kula exchange itself?","hard",["It was considered improper behavior","It set the value of necklaces","It happened only between partners","It was also banned in ordinary trade"],"It was considered improper behavior","Haggling is 'the kind of bargaining the Kula itself was supposed to rule out.'",["A necklace's value comes from its history, not from bargaining.","Lifelong partners are the people the Kula links, and the Kula ruled haggling out.","Ordinary trade was 'bartered with haggling'."])]))
T.append(("A03","Environmental Science","""The Montreal Protocol, signed in 1987, is often called the most successful environmental treaty ever written, and honestly, I think that reputation's earned. Every member of the United Nations eventually ratified it. It phased out chlorofluorocarbons, or CFCs, the chemicals in old refrigerators and spray cans that drift up into the stratosphere and break down ozone. And measurements now show the ozone layer beginning to recover.

So why am I not just celebrating? Two reasons.

The first is time. A CFC molecule released decades ago can stay in the atmosphere for fifty years or more, some types for around a century. So even though production has basically stopped, the chlorine already up there keeps doing damage. The Antarctic ozone hole still opens every spring, and current projections don't have it fully healing until somewhere around the 2060s. A ban changes what goes into the air. It doesn't instantly change what's already there.

The second reason is more interesting. What replaced CFCs? Over time, largely a family of chemicals called HFCs. They contain no chlorine, so they're harmless to ozone. Problem solved, right? Well, no. Many HFCs turned out to be extremely powerful greenhouse gases, some thousands of times more potent than carbon dioxide, molecule for molecule. The fix for one problem was quietly feeding another.

To its credit, the treaty system responded. In 2016, countries agreed on the Kigali Amendment, which schedules a phase-down of HFCs as well. So the story still ends reasonably well.

But notice what happened. The original choice was judged against a single question: does this harm the ozone layer? HFCs passed that test perfectly. Nobody pressed the broader question until the replacements were already in millions of air conditioners. When you swap out a harmful substance, you have to judge its replacement against every problem, not only the one you're trying to solve.""",[
 Q("What is the main purpose of the lecture?","medium",["To qualify the high praise given to the Montreal Protocol","To argue that the Montreal Protocol failed to protect ozone","To explain the chemistry of how chlorine destroys ozone","To compare the Montreal Protocol with climate treaties"],"To qualify the high praise given to the Montreal Protocol","The professor calls the reputation 'earned' and then asks, 'So why am I not just celebrating? Two reasons.'",["The ozone layer is 'beginning to recover'.","The chemistry is not explained beyond CFCs breaking down ozone.","No other climate treaty is compared; Kigali is an amendment to the same protocol."]),
 Q("According to the professor, why does the Antarctic ozone hole still open every spring?","medium",["Chlorine from long-lived CFCs remains in the air","Some countries have still never ratified the treaty","HFCs have begun to break down ozone as well","CFC production rose again after the year 2016"],"Chlorine from long-lived CFCs remains in the air","CFCs can stay up 'fifty years or more', so 'the chlorine already up there keeps doing damage.'",["'Every member of the United Nations eventually ratified it.'","HFCs 'contain no chlorine, so they're harmless to ozone.'","Production 'has basically stopped'; 2016 is the Kigali Amendment."]),
 Q("Why does the professor say 'Problem solved, right?'","hard",["To set up the point that the substitutes caused a new problem","To confirm that HFCs ended all the harm done by older coolants","To suggest that HFCs still damage the ozone layer slowly","To question whether CFCs were ever really harmful at all"],"To set up the point that the substitutes caused a new problem","It is followed at once by 'Well, no. Many HFCs turned out to be extremely powerful greenhouse gases.'",["The professor immediately answers 'Well, no.'","HFCs are 'harmless to ozone'; their harm is to climate.","CFCs are described as breaking down ozone."]),
 Q("What does the professor imply was the mistake in adopting HFCs?","hard",["They were judged against only a single standard","They were never tested for harm to ozone","They were adopted before CFCs were banned","They cooled less well than the CFCs had"],"They were judged against only a single standard","'The original choice was judged against a single question: does this harm the ozone layer?'",["They were tested on ozone and 'passed that test perfectly'.","HFCs are described as what 'replaced CFCs'.","The flaw named is the narrow test, not how well they cooled."])]))
T.append(("A04","Music History","""Here's a problem that bothered musicians for centuries, and it comes straight out of arithmetic. Take a note and go up by a pure fifth, the interval where the frequencies are in a simple three-to-two ratio. Do that twelve times and you should, in theory, land back on your starting note, just seven octaves higher. You don't. You overshoot, by roughly a quarter of a semitone. Pure fifths and pure octaves can't be made to close the circle.

So any keyboard tuning is a compromise. Somewhere, some intervals have to be bent. Older tunings kept the most common intervals pure and pushed the leftover error into keys composers rarely used. Play in those keys and certain chords sounded so harsh that musicians nicknamed one of the bad intervals the wolf, because it howled.

The modern solution, equal temperament, spreads the error evenly. Every fifth is shaved by the same tiny amount, so every key sounds alike, and you can play in any of them. That's the tuning on nearly every piano today.

You've probably heard that Bach wrote The Well-Tempered Clavier, a set of preludes and fugues in all twenty-four major and minor keys, to show off equal temperament. That's the textbook version. But many scholars now doubt it. Well-tempered isn't the same thing as equally tempered. There were tunings in Bach's day that made every key usable while still leaving them slightly different, so some keys sounded calm and pure and others tenser. If Bach had one of those in mind, his collection wasn't proving that all keys are the same. It was showing that all keys were playable, each with its own character.

We can't be certain which tuning Bach used; he never wrote it down. But the point is worth holding onto: something was lost when every key started to sound the same.""",[
 Q("What is the lecture mainly about?","medium",["How keyboard tunings handle an unavoidable mismatch","How Bach invented equal temperament for his keyboard","Why pure fifths sound better than pure octaves do","Why some composers avoided writing in minor keys"],"How keyboard tunings handle an unavoidable mismatch","Pure fifths and octaves 'can't be made to close the circle', so 'any keyboard tuning is a compromise', and the lecture compares the compromises.",["The professor doubts that Bach's collection was even about equal temperament.","Both are called pure; neither is ranked above the other.","Rarely used keys are mentioned, but minor keys are not singled out."]),
 Q("Why does the professor mention the interval called the wolf?","medium",["To show how harsh the leftover error could sound","To describe a tuning that made all keys sound alike","To name the interval equal temperament keeps pure","To explain how Bach chose keys for his collection"],"To show how harsh the leftover error could sound","In older tunings the error went into rare keys, where chords 'sounded so harsh' that one interval was nicknamed the wolf 'because it howled.'",["The wolf belongs to older tunings, which did not make keys alike.","Equal temperament shaves 'every fifth' and keeps none pure.","Bach's collection uses all twenty-four keys; the wolf is not linked to it."]),
 Q("What view of The Well-Tempered Clavier does the professor present?","hard",["It may have shown that every key was playable but distinct","It proves that Bach tuned his keyboard in equal temperament","It was written only in the keys that were most commonly used","It was meant to display the sound of the wolf interval"],"It may have shown that every key was playable but distinct","'It was showing that all keys were playable, each with its own character', with the hedge 'We can't be certain.'",["This is the textbook version that 'many scholars now doubt'.","It is in 'all twenty-four major and minor keys'.","Well temperaments made 'every key usable', so no key had a wolf to display."]),
 Q("What does the professor imply about equal temperament?","hard",["It cost the keys their individual character","It left some keys too harsh for composers to use","It has been replaced on most modern pianos","It keeps every fifth in a perfectly pure ratio"],"It cost the keys their individual character","'Something was lost when every key started to sound the same.'",["Harsh keys belonged to the older tunings.","It is 'the tuning on nearly every piano today'.","'Every fifth is shaved by the same tiny amount.'"])]))
T.append(("A05","Materials Engineering","""During the Second World War, American shipyards built more than two thousand Liberty ships, cargo vessels put together at astonishing speed. Part of that speed came from welding. Instead of riveting steel plates together, as shipbuilders traditionally had, the yards welded them, which was faster and needed less skilled labor.

Then the ships started cracking. Many developed serious fractures, and some broke in two. One welded tanker from the same program split apart while it was tied up at the dock, in calm water. So the obvious suspect was the welding: bad welds, rushed work, done by people who'd only just learned the trade.

And poor welds did play a part. But here's what investigators eventually worked out, and it's the real lesson. Many fractures happened in cold water, in steel that passed the standard tests at room temperature. The researcher Constance Tipper, working at Cambridge, showed that the steel itself behaved differently as it got colder. Above a certain temperature it was ductile: under stress it would stretch and bend before it failed. Below that temperature it turned brittle, and a crack could race through it like a crack through glass, with very little warning.

So why did welding matter? Partly because a riveted hull is made of separate plates, and a crack running through one plate tends to stop at its edge. A welded hull is effectively one continuous piece of steel. Once a crack got going, there was nothing to stop it. Square corners on the hatch openings made things worse by concentrating stress, giving cracks a place to start.

So the failure wasn't one mistake. It was steel that turned brittle in the cold, a building method that let cracks run, and a design that helped them begin. Testing at room temperature had told engineers almost nothing about the conditions the ships actually sailed in.""",[
 Q("What is the main point of the lecture?","medium",["The cracking came from several causes acting together","Poor welding alone explains why the ships cracked","Steel that passed room-temperature tests was safe at sea","Constance Tipper developed a new and stronger steel"],"The cracking came from several causes acting together","'The failure wasn't one mistake': brittle steel, a method that let cracks run, and a design that helped them begin.",["Poor welds 'did play a part', but the professor calls this the obvious suspect, not the whole answer.","Steel that passed those tests still fractured in cold water.","Tipper 'showed that the steel itself behaved differently' in the cold; she did not develop a new steel."]),
 Q("Why does the professor mention a tanker that split apart at the dock?","hard",["To show that the cracks did not need rough seas","To show that welding was faster than riveting","To show that the tanker's steel was still ductile","To show that most fractures began in warm water"],"To show that the cracks did not need rough seas","It broke 'while it was tied up at the dock, in calm water', a striking sign of how serious the cracking was.",["Speed of welding is made earlier and separately.","A ductile steel would 'stretch and bend before it failed', not split.","'Many fractures happened in cold water.'"]),
 Q("According to the professor, why could a crack spread farther in a welded hull?","medium",["The hull was in effect one continuous piece of steel","The welds made the steel brittle at room temperature","The square hatch corners stopped cracks at their edges","Each separate plate made the crack change direction"],"The hull was in effect one continuous piece of steel","'A welded hull is effectively one continuous piece of steel. Once a crack got going, there was nothing to stop it.'",["The steel 'passed the standard tests at room temperature'; it turned brittle in the cold.","Square corners gave cracks 'a place to start', not a place to stop.","Separate plates belong to riveted hulls, where cracks 'tend to stop'."]),
 Q("What does the professor imply about the standard tests on the steel?","hard",["They were run under conditions unlike those at sea","They were skipped to save time in the shipyards","They showed brittleness that builders then ignored","They were reliable only for riveted ship hulls"],"They were run under conditions unlike those at sea","'Testing at room temperature had told engineers almost nothing about the conditions the ships actually sailed in.'",["The steel 'passed the standard tests', so they were run.","It passed them; they did not reveal brittleness.","The tests are faulted for temperature, not hull type."])]))
T.append(("A06","Marine Biology","""Of all the questions in marine biology, one of the oldest is embarrassingly basic: where do eels come from? Aristotle couldn't find eggs or young in them and suggested eels arose from mud. For centuries afterward nobody had a convincing answer, because the eels living in European rivers never seem to breed there.

The breakthrough came from a Danish biologist, Johannes Schmidt, in the early 1900s, and his method was patient and quite clever. Young eel larvae are small, transparent, leaf-shaped creatures that drift in the open ocean, and they grow as they drift. So Schmidt spent years towing fine nets across the Atlantic and recording the size of the larvae he caught. The farther west and south he went, the smaller they got. The very smallest turned up in the Sargasso Sea, a region of the western Atlantic thousands of kilometers from Europe. Smaller larvae mean more recently hatched larvae, so he concluded that's where the eels spawn.

It's a beautiful piece of reasoning. But notice what kind of reasoning it is. It's an inference from a gradient. In the century since, as far as I know, nobody has seen wild European eels spawning or collected their eggs in the Sargasso. Adult eels heading out to sea have been fitted with tags, and some have been tracked a long way in roughly the right direction, but the final scene has never been witnessed.

Does that mean Schmidt was wrong? I'd be surprised. The larval evidence has held up, and nothing better has come along. But be clear about the kind of confidence we have. It's a detective's confidence when every clue points to one suspect, but nobody actually saw the crime.

And that gap matters in practice, because the European eel is now critically endangered, and it's hard to protect a breeding ground you've never actually observed.""",[
 Q("How did Schmidt identify the eels' likely spawning area?","medium",["By finding where the smallest larvae were caught","By following tagged adult eels out to the open sea","By collecting eel eggs in the western Atlantic","By watching eels spawn in European rivers"],"By finding where the smallest larvae were caught","'The very smallest turned up in the Sargasso Sea... so he concluded that's where the eels spawn.'",["Tagging came later and has not reached 'the final scene'.","Nobody has 'collected their eggs in the Sargasso'.","European eels 'never seem to breed there'."]),
 Q("Why does the professor mention Aristotle?","medium",["To show how long eel breeding has puzzled people","To show that eels hatch from eggs buried in mud","To credit him with first describing eel larvae","To contrast his view with the later tagging results"],"To show how long eel breeding has puzzled people","Aristotle 'couldn't find eggs or young', and 'for centuries afterward nobody had a convincing answer.'",["Aristotle said eels 'arose from mud' precisely because he found no eggs.","He found no young at all; Schmidt studied the larvae.","Tagging is discussed later, not set against Aristotle."]),
 Q("What is the professor's main point about Schmidt's conclusion?","hard",["It is well supported but rests on indirect evidence","It has been confirmed by watching eels spawn","It was overturned by later studies of tagged eels","It relied more on luck than on careful scientific reasoning"],"It is well supported but rests on indirect evidence","'It's an inference from a gradient'; 'the larval evidence has held up', but 'nobody actually saw the crime.'",["'Nobody has seen wild European eels spawning.'","Tagged eels went 'in roughly the right direction'; 'I'd be surprised' if Schmidt were wrong.","The professor calls it 'a beautiful piece of reasoning'."]),
 Q("What does the professor imply about protecting the European eel?","hard",["It is harder because spawning has never been observed","It is no longer urgent since eel numbers have recovered","It is simple now that tags have shown where eels spawn","It matters less than finding more larvae near Europe"],"It is harder because spawning has never been observed","'It's hard to protect a breeding ground you've never actually observed.'",["The eel 'is now critically endangered'.","Tags show only 'roughly the right direction'; the final scene 'has never been witnessed'.","The professor ties protection to the breeding ground, and the smallest larvae are far from Europe."])]))
out=[]
for tid,subj,text,qs in T:
    passage="Transcript: "+text
    for i,q in enumerate(qs,1):
        assert q["correct_answer"] in q["choices"] and len(q["choices"])==4 and len(set(q["choices"]))==4
        dist=[c for c in q["choices"] if c!=q["correct_answer"]]
        assert len(dist)==3 and len(q["rats"])==3
        out.append({"id":f"TD2-{tid}-{i}","type":"multiple_choice","passage":passage,"passageGroupId":f"td2-{tid}","prompt":f"[Academic Talk — {subj}] {q['prompt']}","choices":q["choices"],"correct_answer":q["correct_answer"],"difficulty":q["difficulty"],"listeningTask":"academic_talk","explanation":q["explanation"],"distractor_rationales":[{"choice":c,"reason":r} for c,r in zip(dist,q["rats"])],"blanks":None,"graphic":None,"correct_answers":None,"acceptable_answers":None})
json.dump(out,open('items-a.json','w'),indent=1,ensure_ascii=False)
for tid,subj,text,qs in T: print(tid,subj,len(text.split()))
a=out;lo=sh=0
for x in a:
    L=[len(c) for c in x["choices"]];mx=max(L);mn=min(L)
    if len(x["correct_answer"])==mx and L.count(mx)==1: lo+=1; print(" longest:",x["id"])
    if len(x["correct_answer"])==mn and L.count(mn)==1: sh+=1; print(" shortest:",x["id"])
print(len(a),"longest",lo,"shortest",sh)
